A||N||4||Cozmo||Changing 2.8 head gasket||||5||
Z||000000||Cozmo||03-20-2001||10:39 AM||||Workin' on the Targa...<BR>The symptoms are slight miss any RPM, steam from exhaust after warmup. No water in oil, no apparent overpressure in cooling system.<P>OK, after doing<BR> a vacuum test (21, kinda wobbly)<BR> a compression test (all cyls 150 except #5 180)<BR> and a leakdown test (all cyls &lt;5% except #5 20% with bubbling heard in the cooling system)<BR>I think #5 (back L.H. corner near trunk) is leaking between the cyl. and water jacket.<BR>I've pulled it apart down to the heads themselves, I'll remove them this weekend.<P>The engine was rebuilt very recently by the PO (it shows it-very clean inside)<BR>I'd like to do this repair cheep and easy. When I pull the heads, how can I tell if it's a blown gasket or a warped/cracked head? I know that I should have the heads reconditioned, but I have to watch the $$. A Felpro gasket set is $70 and new head bolts are $25.<BR> Is there any way I can check the heads myself? It'll cost me $225 to get them done at the machine shop and I'd like to avoid that cost because they were just done. BUT I don't want to waste $100 worth of gaskets and bolts.<BR>  I'm just learning this mechanic art, any suggestions on how I can do this inexpensive and relible?<BR>Thanks,<P>------------------<BR>Cozmo<BR>'85 2M6 Targa<BR>'86 GT<BR>'87 Coupe||||reg||5
Z||000001||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||10:49 AM||||well, about the best you can do is to check for warpage. If the head is cracked you might also get lucky enough to visually see the crack, but if it's that cracked then chances are there are other micro cracks hidden around. If you suspect the gasket the check the cylinder punchout on the gasket to see if there is any evidence of combustion leakage evident typically by carbon buildup on the gasket surface.||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||03-20-2001||10:58 AM||||Sounds like you really did your homework with your diagnosis.  Good job. It is probably only the head gasket but I would have the heads resurfaced. I think the price you mentioned seems high.  You might check around to find a better price.  They also might check the head for you to see if it is warped for only a little cash. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cozmo||03-20-2001||11:14 AM||||Thanks skitime, the price quoted was for a "full" head recondition (valves, springs etc). I'll go by the machine shop and see what they charge for a leak check and resurfacing this afternoon.<P>862M4inCA, I'll look real close at the heads when I get them off for clues. I understand that examining the gaskets can reveal a lot of info, I'll mike them and look for oddities like you suggested.<BR>Wow..<BR>I did searches on this Forum and the rest of the web on head stuff, there is a huge amount of info out there (too much!)<BR>Thanks,<BR>C02M0<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||03-20-2001||03:56 PM||||Cosmo,<P>I would think that, with the amount of leackage your reporting, the "offending" problem would be easily seen when you remove the head. As a quick-fix, why not pull a head off of the broken GT, install some new valve seals and slap that puppy on the targa? Of course, if the previous owner paid someone to do a bunch of work the the targa heads, you'd wind up with one really good head and one that my need a valve job. I guess you could do a compression check on the GT and verify that there is little to no hiss out of the intake or exhaust. That may buy you a little confidence and time.<P>Just some random thoughts,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
